{
  "beruf": "elektro-technik",
  "title": "Agriculture and farm work",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "spanish-for-work run, 2026-11-07: written by hand for Spain, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon or in another deck are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Many newcomers start in el campo: greenhouses in Almería, strawberries in Huelva, fruit in Lleida and Murcia, olives in Jaén, the grape harvest in La Rioja and La Mancha. This deck has the tools and machines, the jobs, the season and the pay, safety in the heat and with chemicals, and how to talk with the team. In a cuadrilla, nearly everyone says tú.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work on a farm or in a greenhouse in Spain? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Temporero/a",
    "Jornalero/a",
    "Peón agrícola",
    "Recolector/a",
    "Mozo/a de almacén de fruta",
    "Tractorista"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Ask on the first day how you are paid: per day (el jornal), per hour or per box (a destajo). Write down your days and your boxes.",
    "In the summer, start early, drink water often and rest in the shade. If someone feels dizzy or stops making sense, call 112. Heat can kill.",
    "Don't go into an area that has just been sprayed. Ask: «¿Se puede entrar ya?»",
    "Farm contracts usually come from the sector's collective agreement (convenio) for your province, and they can differ a lot. A union such as CCOO or UGT can read yours with you. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
  "links": [
